---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// PLACEHOLDER: the address below is the one the site already used; set the real one before launch.
const contact = 'mailto:services@openreplicant.example?subject=Mapping%20and%20optimisation%20engagement';
const fernhillReport = 'https://github.com/OpenReplicant/Morphogen/blob/main/demos/fernhill/engagement/report.md';
const fernhillDemo = 'https://github.com/OpenReplicant/Morphogen/tree/main/demos/fernhill';
---

<BaseLayout title="Services | Open Replicant">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="splash">
      <div class="orb orb--a" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="orb orb--b" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__content">
      <span class="badge badge--open-source">For small and medium businesses</span>
      <h1>Find where the margin goes</h1>
      <p class="hero__tagline">
        We map your business and its systems from your own documents, data and
        people, find the opportunities to do better, and write each one up as an
        implementation project with a target and a measure. Then we check the
        result against that measure, with a judge nobody on the project can tune.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a href={contact} class="btn btn--large">Talk to us</a>
        <a href="#sample" class="btn btn--outline btn--large">Read a sample report</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>A map</h3>
        <p>
          Your firm as plain files: teams, the steps a job goes through, the
          tools behind them and what is handed between them. Every claim cites
          the document line or interview quote it rests on, so you can check it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>An opportunity report</h3>
        <p>
          The numbers that matter, computed from your data by a script you can
          rerun: where money, time or customers are lost, and which stated
          beliefs the data does not bear out.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Implementation projects</h3>
        <p>
          Each opportunity written up as a project: what changes, the target and
          how it is measured, what it is worth, the options compared, cost, risk,
          and what must not change.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Judged results</h3>
        <p>
          Where a change can be tested, Morphogen tests it on cases it was not
          tuned on and records a pass or fail in an evidence log. You see the
          verdict, not just our opinion of it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>A first call.</strong> What the business does, what worries you,
        and what must not change: the margin you hold, the tools your people
        will actually use, the budget, the payback you need.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your material.</strong> The documents you already have (handbook,
        price sheets, job or order exports, a systems list) and an interview
        with whoever runs the place. Access to systems only where you grant it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The map and the baselines.</strong> We map the firm with
        Morphogen's kit, tie every claim to its source, and compute the
        baselines from your data with scripts you keep.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Options, researched.</strong> For each part worth changing we
        catalogue the alternatives (tools, settings, ways of working) with what
        their vendors actually say, quoted, and what still needs a trial.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Judged where it can be.</strong> A spreadsheet formula, a
        setting, a rule or a prompt can become a Morphogen cell: searched for a
        better version, then judged on held-out cases it never saw.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The report.</strong> Projects in the order we would do them,
        each with a target and a measure. You decide what goes ahead; we can
        carry projects out with you and judge the result against its target.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What makes it different</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>A judge the work can't game</h3>
        <p>
          The rules are fixed before the search starts, the measurements are
          taken outside the thing being changed, and the test cases are held
          back from whoever is optimising. No judge is perfect; this one only
          gets stricter, and every verdict goes into a log that cannot be
          quietly edited.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Measured outcomes</h3>
        <p>
          Every project has a number to move and the place that number comes
          from (your job sheet, your accounts, your timesheets). If it does not
          move, you will see that too.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Open source</h3>
        <p>
          Morphogen is Apache-2.0 and on GitHub. The map, the scripts and the
          evidence are files you keep. Anyone can rerun the numbers, and you can
          carry on without us.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/OpenReplicant/Morphogen" class="btn btn--ghost">Morphogen on GitHub</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow" id="sample">
    <h2>A sample report: Fernhill Kitchens</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Fernhill is a made-up 11-person kitchen firm; its documents, job sheet and
      interview were written for the demo. The method, the measurements and the
      verdict are real runs of Morphogen on that material.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="excerpt">
      “The firm quotes 35% on cost and gets 16%. The quote calculator's costs
      are too low on every one of the 50 completed jobs, by a median 14%,
      because its constants date from March 2023. That is about
      <strong>£84,000 of margin over those 50 jobs, about £53,000 a year</strong>.
      Corrected constants, found by search and checked on 19 jobs held back
      from that search, bring the calculator's error from 12.4% to 3.4%.”
    </blockquote>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Project</th><th>Target and measure</th><th>Status in the demo</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>1. A quote calculator that knows what jobs cost</td>
            <td>Cost within 5% of actual on completed jobs it was not fitted to</td>
            <td>Judged: 12.4% to 3.4% error on 19 held-out jobs, pass (7 runs, sign test p = 0.008)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>2. Quotes in three days</td>
            <td>Median survey-to-quote 3 days (now 9); win rate by days to quote, monthly</td>
            <td>Proposed; win rate falls from 80% within 5 days to none of 16 at 15 days or more</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>3. A schedule the fitters can see</td>
            <td>Late fitting starts under 20% (now 48%); invoice 2 days after sign-off (now 12.5)</td>
            <td>Four options catalogued from vendor pages; a trial plan, none measured yet</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4. Fewer remakes</td>
            <td>Remade cabinets (now 6 of 50 jobs)</td>
            <td>Smaller, later</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <a href={fernhillReport} class="btn btn--large">Read the full report</a>
      <a href={fernhillDemo} class="btn btn--ghost btn--large">See the demo's files</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Engagement shapes</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Prices depend on the size of the firm and how much material there is. We
      quote a fixed price after the first call.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Opportunity scan</h3>
        <p class="price"><span class="tbd">$TBD</span></p>
        <ul>
          <li>The mapping run on what is public about your firm</li>
          <li>A first list of likely opportunities</li>
          <li>A call to go through it</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Map and opportunity report</h3>
        <p class="price"><span class="tbd">$TBD</span></p>
        <ul>
          <li>An interview, and your documents and data</li>
          <li>The map, the baselines and their scripts</li>
          <li>Options catalogued; one change judged where possible</li>
          <li>The report, written up as implementation projects</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Implementation project</h3>
        <p class="price"><span class="tbd">$TBD</span> per project</p>
        <ul>
          <li>One project from the report, carried out with you</li>
          <li>Trials of the options before you choose</li>
          <li>The result measured against its target</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h3>Ongoing measurement</h3>
        <p class="price"><span class="tbd">$TBD</span> / month</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The baselines rerun on new data, quarterly</li>
          <li>Drift caught before it costs margin</li>
          <li>Changes judged before they are used</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section center narrow">
    <h2>Start with a call</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Tell us what the business does and what you would like to be better.
      We will say plainly whether we can help.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a href={contact} class="btn btn--large">Email us</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
